# Kettle Linux: the name Valve's arm64 Steam gives the SoC's video encoder (iris), as on the Steam
# Frame: the Remote Play host's encoder opens /dev/video-enc0 and nothing else. Steam's encoding is
# untested.
# The decoder keeps only its /dev/video* name, which Remote Play's client (streaming_client) finds
# by itself. Not /dev/video-dec0: Chromium (Steam's UI, steamwebhelper) looks for decoders by that
# name only, and its V4L2 decoder doesn't work with iris: on the UI's videos its GPU process spun
# about 3.4 cores until it crashed (SIGTRAP), every few minutes, and the UI reloaded.
SUBSYSTEM=="video4linux", ATTR{name}=="qcom-iris-encoder", SYMLINK+="video-enc0"
